= ψ Loss Tangent =

{{Plain_Hook
| in_one_sentence    = The ψ loss tangent tan δ_ψ = ε''_ψ/ε'_ψ is the fraction of ψ field energy dissipated per radian of oscillation — setting a hard ceiling on the resonator quality factor Q_ψ = 1/tan δ_ψ and therefore on the soliton lifetime, the Anchor burst duration, and ultimately on the operational effectiveness of every [[Psi-Tech]] device that relies on sustained ψ field coherence.
| everyday_analogy   = A tuning fork struck in air rings for several seconds — its internal damping is very low. The same fork struck while submerged in honey stops almost immediately — the high damping dissipates energy instantly. ψ loss tangent measures exactly this: how much energy the composite material absorbs from the ψ field per oscillation cycle. A high-loss-tangent material is honey for the ψ field; a low-loss-tangent material (like quartz) lets the ψ resonance ring freely.
| what_it_predicts   = (1) A composite loss tangent dominated by whichever phase has the highest loss — requiring the low-loss quartz scaffold to be the largest volume fraction; (2) A Q ceiling <math>Q_\psi^{\rm max} = 1/\tan\delta_\psi</math> that cannot be exceeded regardless of cavity geometry; (3) A minimum Anchor burst soliton lifetime of <math>\tau_{\rm min} = 2Q_\psi/\omega</math> — at Q = 200 and 40 kHz, this is 1.6 ms ringdown, sufficient for the 90-second Anchor mode operation; (4) A frequency-dependent loss profile in BST that is much lower at 40 kHz than at microwave frequencies, justifying the low-frequency operating strategy.
| if_you_remember_one_thing = The loss tangent sets the Q ceiling: Q_ψ ≤ 1/tan δ_ψ. Every design decision that increases tan δ_ψ directly lowers the maximum achievable Q. The quartz filler in the composite is present specifically to dilute the higher-loss BST and MWCNT phases and keep the composite tan δ_ψ below 0.005, which is the threshold for Q > 200.
}}

== Definition and Physical Meaning ==

The complex ψ permittivity from [[ψ Constitutive Relations]] (B1):

:<math>\varepsilon_\psi(\omega) = \varepsilon'_\psi(\omega) - i\varepsilon''_\psi(\omega)</math>

The '''ψ loss tangent''' is defined as the ratio of imaginary to real parts:

:<math>\boxed{\tan\delta_\psi \equiv \frac{\varepsilon''_\psi(\omega)}{\varepsilon'_\psi(\omega)}}</math>

{{Math_Bridge
| equation_label = ψ loss tangent
| equation       = <math>\tan\delta_\psi = \frac{\varepsilon''_\psi}{\varepsilon'_\psi}</math>
| in_english     =
* <math>\varepsilon'_\psi</math> — real (storage) part of permittivity: how much ψ energy is held temporarily by the material and returned to the field. This is lossless.
* <math>\varepsilon''_\psi</math> — imaginary (loss) part: how much ψ energy is permanently dissipated per cycle, converted to thermal energy or structural disorder.
* <math>\tan\delta_\psi = 0</math>: perfectly lossless material — ψ oscillates indefinitely with no decay.
* <math>\tan\delta_\psi = 1</math>: energy dissipated equals energy stored each cycle — field decays to <math>1/e</math> in one radian of oscillation.
* <math>\tan\delta_\psi \ll 1</math>: low-loss material — field rings for many cycles before decaying. Required for [[ψ Resonator]] and [[Psi Stabilizer]] applications.
}}

=== Attenuation Length ===

In a medium with complex wave vector <math>k = k' + ik''</math>, the ψ field amplitude decays as <math>e^{-k''x}</math>. For small loss (<math>\tan\delta_\psi \ll 1</math>):

:<math>k'' \approx \frac{\omega}{2v_\psi}\tan\delta_\psi</math>

The attenuation length (distance over which amplitude falls to <math>1/e</math>):

:<math>\alpha_\psi^{-1} = \frac{1}{k''} \approx \frac{2v_\psi}{\omega\tan\delta_\psi} = \frac{\lambda_\psi}{\pi\tan\delta_\psi}</math>

where <math>\lambda_\psi = 2\pi v_\psi/\omega</math> is the ψ wavelength. For <math>\tan\delta_\psi = 0.005</math> at 40 kHz:

:<math>\alpha_\psi^{-1} = \frac{\lambda_\psi}{\pi \times 0.005} \approx 64\,\lambda_\psi</math>

The field propagates 64 wavelengths before attenuating by <math>1/e</math> — well within the design margin for the 20 cm [[Psi Stabilizer]] shell.

=== Q Ceiling from Material Loss ===

For a resonator filled with material of loss tangent <math>\tan\delta_\psi</math>, the material-limited quality factor is:

:<math>Q_\psi^{\rm (material)} = \frac{1}{\tan\delta_\psi}</math>

The total loaded Q (including radiation and coupling losses) satisfies:

:<math>\frac{1}{Q_L} = \frac{1}{Q_{\rm material}} + \frac{1}{Q_{\rm radiation}} + \frac{1}{Q_{\rm coupling}}</math>

Since <math>Q_L \leq Q_{\rm material} = 1/\tan\delta_\psi</math> always, the loss tangent sets an absolute upper bound on achievable resonator Q regardless of geometry. For the [[Psi Stabilizer]] target <math>Q_\psi > 200</math>:

:<math>\tan\delta_\psi < \frac{1}{200} = 0.005 \qquad \text{(design requirement)}</math>

=== Ringdown Time ===

The e-folding time of the ψ field amplitude after the drive is cut:

:<math>\tau_{\rm ringdown} = \frac{2Q_\psi}{\omega}</math>

At the [[Psi Stabilizer]] design point (<math>Q_\psi = 200</math>, <math>\omega = 2\pi \times 40\,\text{kHz}</math>):

:<math>\tau_{\rm ringdown} = \frac{2 \times 200}{2\pi \times 4\times10^4} \approx 1.6\,\text{ms}</math>

The ψ field persists for 1.6 ms after the drive ceases. This is orders of magnitude shorter than the 90-second Anchor burst — the drive must be continuous during Anchor mode to maintain the field amplitude. Soliton formation (from the <math>-\lambda\psi^3</math> term) provides additional amplitude stabilisation, but does not replace continuous drive at this Q value.

Higher Q reduces the required drive power at the expense of narrower bandwidth — the design Q > 200 is chosen to balance drive power against frequency selectivity.

== Composite Loss Budget ==

The total composite loss tangent is a weighted average of the phase contributions. For a series loss model (each phase adds its contribution):

:<math>\tan\delta_\psi^{\rm (composite)} \approx \sum_i f_i\,\tan\delta_{\psi,i}</math>

where <math>f_i</math> is the volume fraction and <math>\tan\delta_{\psi,i}</math> is the loss tangent of phase <math>i</math>.

