The composite loss budget as formulated nominally exceeds the Q > 200 design target. Three optimisation levers are available:

# '''Increase quartz fraction''' from 25% to 35%, reducing MWCNT-epoxy to 10% and acrylate binder to 7%. New estimated total: ~0.0055 — marginal improvement.
# '''Switch to lower-loss photopolymer''' scaffold. Formlabs Rigid 10K (loss tangent ~0.003 at 40 kHz) instead of High Temp Resin. New estimated total: ~0.0051.
# '''Use PVDF instead of PZT-5H.''' PVDF has lower mechanical loss tangent (~0.005) than unsintered PZT powder. New estimated total: ~0.0047 — below the 0.005 target.

The recommended formulation for production shells: quartz 35%, Rigid 10K scaffold 25%, PVDF 8%, BST 5%, MWCNT-epoxy 12%, ferrofluid channels 5%, residual voids 10%. Predicted composite tan δ_ψ ≈ 0.0045 < 0.005 target.

=== Frequency Dependence of BST Loss ===

The loss tangent of BST is strongly frequency-dependent. At microwave frequencies (1–10 GHz), BST has <math>\tan\delta \approx 0.05</math>–<math>0.10</math> — too lossy for high-Q resonators. However, at low frequencies:

{| class="wikitable" style="width:100%;"
! Frequency !! BST <math>\tan\delta</math> (approximate) !! Source
|-
| 10 Hz || ~0.001 || Impedance analyser measurement (published literature)
|-
| 1 kHz || ~0.003 || Impedance analyser
|-
| 40 kHz || ~0.010–0.020 || Estimated by extrapolation; confirmed at TML-1
|-
| 1 MHz || ~0.025 || Impedance analyser
|-
| 1 GHz || ~0.050–0.100 || Microwave measurement (literature)
|}

Operating at 40 kHz rather than microwave frequencies reduces the BST contribution to composite loss by approximately 5×. This is the primary engineering rationale for the low-frequency operating strategy — not just biological coupling considerations, but also the fundamental materials advantage of operating where BST loss is minimal.

