== ψ-Dependent Extensions ==

=== Coherence-Dependent Loss in Biological Tissue ===

Within the Track B framework, the loss tangent of biological cortical tissue may depend on neural coherence state. In a high-coherence state, the operator's cortical ψ emission pattern is more ordered — the source distribution <math>J_\psi^{\rm (bio)}</math> has higher spatial coherence and less cancellation between out-of-phase contributions from different cortical columns.

From a materials perspective, high coherence corresponds to lower effective loss tangent in the tissue: the tissue responds more collectively and stores ψ energy more efficiently. If true, <math>\tan\delta_\psi^{\rm (bio)}</math> decreases during HRV-coherent states — which would manifest as an increased effective Q of the [[Psi Stabilizer]] resonator when the operator reaches peak coherence. This would be observable as a narrowing of the S11 resonance peak.

This prediction is testable at TML-3: measure resonator S11 bandwidth at baseline HRV vs peak coherence. A statistically significant decrease in bandwidth (increase in Q) would support the coherence-dependent loss hypothesis.

=== Soliton Amplitude Stabilisation and Effective Loss Reduction ===

The <math>-\lambda\psi^3</math> nonlinear term supports soliton solutions that are self-sustaining — they do not decay exponentially as a linear field would. In the energy budget sense, the soliton extracts energy from the drive and uses it to compensate the material loss, maintaining a stable amplitude envelope. This can be thought of as an effective reduction of <math>\tan\delta_\psi</math> for the soliton mode relative to a linear wave of the same frequency.

The effective Q of a soliton mode in a nonlinear medium is higher than the linear Q = 1/tan δ_ψ — the soliton self-organises to minimise its interaction with the loss mechanism. This is the mathematical basis for the [[Psi Stabilizer]] Anchor mode being a fundamentally different operating regime from linear emission: the nonlinear λ term provides partial compensation of material loss that a linear analysis would not predict.

== TML Measurement Protocol ==

=== TML-1: Loss Tangent Extraction ===

'''Method 1 — VNA S-parameter (preferred for composite samples):'''

# Mount composite tablet (10 mm × 2 mm) in coaxial fixture with gold electrode contacts.
# Measure S21 transmission from 1 kHz to 1 MHz.
# Apply Nicholson–Ross–Weir extraction: complex <math>\varepsilon_\psi(\omega) = \varepsilon'_\psi - i\varepsilon''_\psi</math> from S11 + S21.
# Compute tan δ_ψ = ε''_ψ/ε'_ψ at each frequency.

'''Method 2 — Ringdown (for assembled shell):'''

# Drive shell resonator at <math>\omega_{010}</math> with a 10-cycle tone burst.
# Cut drive and measure ψ proxy signal (OPM field at shell surface) decay.
# Fit exponential: amplitude ~ <math>e^{-t/\tau}</math>.
# Extract <math>Q_\psi = \omega\tau/2</math>, then <math>\tan\delta_\psi = 1/Q_\psi</math>.

'''Pass criteria:'''
* <math>\tan\delta_\psi < 0.005</math> for optimised composite formulation
* Ringdown time <math>\tau > 1.6</math> ms at 40 kHz (Q > 200)
* Reproducibility: < ±20% variation across three samples from same batch

=== TML-3: Biological Loss Tangent Estimation ===

Measure resonator Q shift between empty cavity and operator-present cavity:

:<math>\frac{1}{Q_L^{\rm (operator)}} = \frac{1}{Q_L^{\rm (empty)}} + \frac{1}{Q_{\rm bio}}</math>

Solving for <math>Q_{\rm bio}</math> gives the Q contribution of the biological tissue alone, from which:

:<math>\tan\delta_\psi^{\rm (bio)} = \frac{1}{Q_{\rm bio}} \times \eta_{\rm fill}^{-1}</math>

where <math>\eta_{\rm fill}</math> is the filling factor (fraction of cavity energy in the biological tissue region), estimated from the ψ₀₁₀ mode shape and operator head dimensions.

